<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Islamic Republic of Pakistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1956 CE – present · capital Islamabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Muslim-majority republic of South Asia, a nuclear power whose history has alternated between civilian and military rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan's</a> first constitution, adopted on 23 March 1956, ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a> and declared an Islamic republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskander-mirza">Iskander Mirza</a> as president. It was abrogated in 1958 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> seized power. The capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> to the newly built city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a> in the 1960s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Military rulers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan">Yahya Khan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq">Zia-ul-Haq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf">Pervez Musharraf</a>, governed for long periods, interspersed with civilian governments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benazir-bhutto">Benazir Bhutto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nawaz-sharif">Nawaz Sharif</a>. Zia promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> fought wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and conducted nuclear tests in 1998. The 1973 constitution, still in force, created a parliamentary federation.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 1971 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bangladesh">East Pakistan</a> seceded after a brutal war and became Bangladesh. The republic continues as a federal parliamentary state of four provinces and other territories, with the army a powerful political force.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/islamic-republic-of-pakistan.jpg" alt="Islamic Republic of Pakistan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Pakistan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1956 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Islamabad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ayub Khan, Zulfikar Ali Bhutto, Benazir Bhutto</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Urdu, English, Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pakistan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=islamic-republic-of-pakistan" aria-label="Islamic Republic of Pakistan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6544 955 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Islamic Republic of Pakistan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6864 1211L6778 1519M6864 1211L7409 1559"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6778 1519h0M7409 1559h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6864 1211h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6864 1211h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Islamabad. <a href="/atlas?year=1991&amp;focus=islamic-republic-of-pakistan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
